Not a terminal, it was decided here: the want was never the grid but
text that opens, file:line, url, hash, and tmux already hands over the
word under the pointer (mouse_word, copy_cursor_word, mouse_hyperlink,
copy-pipe; run-shell expands #{...}), so owning the grid bought none of
it. gio's window owns one all the same, for its shells, a terminal of
its own, st's as sys/vt has it (`gio/term.go`), and finds the word under
a click itself, as tmux would (`outline/click.go:/func Word/`).

Measured on next-3.8: word-separators splits on / : . — the word under
ed/2-motion/04-fwd.go:12 comes back as `motion`. Set it to " " and the
whole address comes back. That one line is the enabler; without it the
extent has to be recomputed from mouse_x against the pattern.

Deferred, named:
  relative  a path is relative to the cwd that printed it, not the cwd
            now. pane_current_path is right until a cd. acme solves it
            by owning the window — the price of not writing one.

Since answered: exec, the sibling verb, text that names an action, run
it, by the middle button, which hands it to the shell's prompt, where
Return runs it (`design/mouse`); and ed, a third front end, whose right
and middle clicks hand their word to p.

What a terminal would still buy is an editable output window. That is a
different want, and capture-pane -p | ed is the cheap half of it.
